.SS Session count vs. logind
.PP
The user count in the header is the number of \fBUSER_PROCESS\fR
entries in \fI/var/run/utmp\fR. As \fButmp\fR(5) notes, "there may
be more users currently using the system, because not all programs
use utmp logging" — so the count can disagree with \fBloginctl
list\-sessions\fR on systemd hosts in either direction. Stale tmux
or screen ptys inflate it; session managers that bypass utmp deflate
it. \fBprocps-ng\fR's \fBw\fR resolves this by linking against
\fBlibsystemd\fR; \fBprocutils\fR currently reads only utmp.
.PP
A missing \fI/var/run/utmp\fR is an empty session table, not a read
error. Other read failures remain errors. With \fB\-h\fR, both the
uptime summary and column headings are suppressed.
.SS Column layout
.PP
Default USER and TTY fields are eight columns; TTY names are limited
to eight characters. The optional FROM field defaults to sixteen
columns. LOGIN@ occupies eight columns; IDLE, JCPU, and PCPU each
occupy seven. \fB\-s\fR omits LOGIN@, JCPU, and PCPU without changing
the remaining column layout.
.SS Foreground process detection
.PP
Without \fB\-u\fR, \fBw\fR identifies each session's foreground
process by walking \fI/proc\fR for processes whose \fItty_nr\fR
matches the session's controlling terminal and picking the one
matching the terminal's \fItpgid\fR. This is best-effort: in
unusual configurations (multiplexers, processes that detach from
their controlling tty, very short-lived foreground processes) the
chosen process may not be the one a user would name. Pass \fB\-u\fR
to fall back to the utmp entry's own login PID.
.SS Idle time
.PP
\fIIDLE\fR is computed from the modification time of the session's
tty device file (\fI/dev/<TTY>\fR). It reflects when the device was
last written to (typically when the user typed something), not when
the foreground process last did anything. A session running a
long-lived process that never reads stdin still shows growing idle
time.
